Fillets: where two roundings that cross at a vertex are cut along their
intersection (PerformTwoCornerbyInter, not on one point of the pivot), the
cut of the larger one by the smaller one's face at the end does not cut a
periodic p-curve of the smaller one's contact to its first period
(mitcad#1428).

The case of 1610: the groove's rim meets a wall at a vertex whose third
edge (the wall with the flange's top) is concave; the rounding along the
wall's arc (a torus) and the one along the flange's top (a cylinder) end
OnSame on that edge at different points and cross. The torus's contact on
the wall is a circle whose p-curve on the plane is a Geom2d_Circle of
range [0, 2 pi]; the contact's parameters there ran from -0.069 (the point
where the cylinder crosses it, found on the 3D curve) to 1.34. The second
intersection, on the p-curve cut to [max(First, w - d), min(Last, w + d)],
was [0, 0.0009], without w: it failed, and PerformMoreThreeCorner filled
the corner with a plate 0.049 mm off the stored body.

- PerformTwoCornerbyInter: a periodic p-curve is taken over
  [w - d, w + d] as it is.

On the design's stored body before the item: each corner cut along the
roundings' intersection, as the stored body's (its faces within 1e-6 mm),
the item's 156 faces.

